From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: log no key codes in winios_drv_post_key

winios_drv_post_key logged the first 8 keys of the process and every
256th with their virtual key, scan code and flags. stderr is the app's
log, in a release build too, and a letter's or digit's virtual key is
its ASCII code: a sign-in typed first was in the log, up to 8
characters of it.

The line now gives the running count and the status only; the count
moves to winios_count_key, which the character post shares.

Linear: PLA-267
Class: host-app
Evidence: tools/tests/test_key_logging.py (host checks; phone gate not run)
Offered-upstream: no; the key path is the iOS driver's
---
 build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++--------------
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c b/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c
index b61041b..c978a2a 100644
--- a/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c
+++ b/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c
@@ -100,6 +100,23 @@ void winios_drv_post_mouse(int x, int y, unsigned int flags, unsigned int mouse_
  * E0 (its HostIO hio_keys.c). Weak: a host without it is not called. */
 extern unsigned int playport_key_scan( unsigned int vk ) __attribute__((weak));
 
+/* ml647: COUNT EVENTS, and never let a cap masquerade as absence. The
+ * old "first 40 lines" cap was exhausted by arrow keys early in the
+ * session, so the WASD presses that prompted this fix left no trace at
+ * all and the log looked like they were never sent. Log the first few,
+ * then one line per 256 with a running total that is always truthful.
+ * Playport: the count and the status only, never which key: a log of the
+ * keys posted is the text typed, a password too. */
+static void winios_count_key( NTSTATUS st )
+{
+    static unsigned cnt, bad;
+    if (st) bad++;
+    cnt++;
+    if (cnt <= 8 || (cnt & 0xff) == 0)
+        dprintf(2, "[winios] ml647 drv_post_key #%u -> status=0x%x (failures=%u)\n",
+                cnt, (unsigned)st, bad);
+}
+
 /* Keyboard sibling of winios_drv_post_key: packages an INPUT_KEYBOARD
  * event. vk is a Windows virtual-key code (VK_RETURN=0x0D, VK_SPACE=0x20,
  * VK_ESCAPE=0x1B, ...); flags is 0 for key-down, KEYEVENTF_KEYUP (0x2)
@@ -149,20 +166,7 @@ void winios_drv_post_key(unsigned short vk, unsigned int flags)
     input.ki.dwExtraInfo = 0;
 
     st = server_send_hardware_message( NULL, 0, &input, 0 );
-    {
-        /* ml647: COUNT EVENTS, and never let a cap masquerade as absence. The
-         * old "first 40 lines" cap was exhausted by arrow keys early in the
-         * session, so the WASD presses that prompted this fix left no trace at
-         * all and the log looked like they were never sent. Log the first few,
-         * then one line per 256 with a running total that is always truthful. */
-        static unsigned cnt, bad;
-        if (st) bad++;
-        cnt++;
-        if (cnt <= 8 || (cnt & 0xff) == 0)
-            dprintf(2, "[winios] ml647 drv_post_key #%u vk=0x%x scan=0x%x flags=0x%x "
-                       "-> status=0x%x (failures=%u)\n",
-                    cnt, vk, scan, flags, (unsigned)st, bad);
-    }
+    winios_count_key( st );
 }
 
 /* [winios-tree] window-tree dump: every top-level window with class,
